# Nothing under data/ is committed by default: the downloaded source pool, the Silesia
# corpus and both scoring corpora are large and third-party.
*
!.gitignore
!benchmark/

# corpus-stage1 and corpus-stage2 are NOT committed here. They live in their own
# repositories and arrive via `just corpus-pull`:
#
#   conjectures-compression-corpus-1   the public set
#   conjectures-compression-corpus-2   the held-out set, private
#
# That split is the point, not an accident of layout. Committing stage 2 here would
# mean this repository could never be made public and could never be forked into the
# miner-facing one -- and because git keeps history, deleting the files later would not
# undo either. Keeping the datasets separate puts the boundary on a repository ACL,
# which can be changed, instead of on this history, which cannot.
#
# So: do not add a whitelist rule for them. If you need to hand stage 2 to a validator
# that cannot clone the private repo, `just corpus-package` still writes an archive.
#
# Still ignored by the `*` above, and must stay that way:
#   benchmark/sources/                 the ~900 MB real-world pool (fetch-corpus-sources.sh)
#   benchmark/silesia*/                the reference corpus used as a control
#   benchmark/silesia*.formats.json    regenerated by download-silesia.sh on every fetch
#   benchmark/corpus-stage{1,2}.formats.json  mirrored by corpus-pull from each dataset
#                                      repo's own formats.json -- same story as the stages
#                                      themselves, just not a whitelist rule away from it
#   benchmark/dist/                    packaged stage 2 archives (`just corpus-package`)
#   benchmark/.corpus-repos/           the dataset checkouts `just corpus-pull` clones into

# corpus-initial: the original five-file corpus, kept because it is what every byte
# count published before the staged benchmark was measured against. Its format-label
# manifest (filename -> label, for the benchmark analysis) is a sibling of the
# directory, not a member, so it needs its own rule.
!benchmark/corpus-initial/
!benchmark/corpus-initial/**
!benchmark/corpus-initial.formats.json
